{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "06df42ab-77ad-4b9e-8bd8-59c2bc275a94",
    "name": "Tasks Management",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json",
    "_exporter_id": "36418423",
    "_collection_link": "https://go.postman.co/collection/36418423-06df42ab-77ad-4b9e-8bd8-59c2bc275a94?source=collection_link",
    "description": "Rafiq Mentorship — Tasks Management API.\n\nSTUDENT SETUP (Dashboard GUI only):\n1. Create your own Supabase project in the Dashboard.\n2. Open SQL Editor, paste the complete schema-redesign.sql from this repository, and run it in the new training project.\n3. Open Edge Functions → Deploy a new function → Via Editor. Create send-invite-email, paste the complete source from supabase/functions/send-invite-email/index.ts, then deploy.\n4. Open Edge Functions → Secrets and add SMTP_HOST, SMTP_PORT, SMTP_USER, SMTP_PASSWORD, and optional SMTP_FROM from your email provider.\n5. Copy Project URL and Publishable key from Project Settings → API Keys (or Connect). Set base_url and api_key in your Postman environment.\n6. Set frontend_origin (for example http://localhost:3000). Sign up and log in; the collection saves the session tokens in the selected environment.\n\nNo Supabase CLI, terminal, Docker, local Supabase stack, or Supabase .env file is required. The frontend may have its own local environment file; do not put SMTP credentials in frontend configuration.\n\nInvitation requests call /functions/v1/send-invite-email. Postman must send Origin using frontend_origin. The function sends email using SMTP secrets saved in the Dashboard. Accept Project Invitation remains a PostgREST RPC."
  },
